    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>A MUD tax in Texas is the property tax charged by a municipal utility district, the local government that paid for the water lines, sewer plant and drainage in many newer Houston-area subdivisions. It shows up as its own line on your tax bill, it can add a meaningful amount to your total tax rate, and it varies widely from one neighborhood to the next, even within the same school district.</p>
<p>If you are buying in Katy, Cypress, Fulshear, Richmond, Spring or another fast-growing suburb, here is how MUDs work, how to find the rate before you sign a contract and how to compare a MUD home with one inside a city.</p>

<h2>What is a municipal utility district?</h2>
<p>A municipal utility district (MUD) is a political subdivision of the State of Texas created to provide water, wastewater and drainage service to an area, often land outside city limits where no city utilities exist. Depending on how it was created, a MUD may also build roads, parks and recreational facilities. MUDs operate under the Texas Water Code, are overseen by the Texas Commission on Environmental Quality (TCEQ) and are governed by a board of directors elected by voters who live in the district.</p>
<p>Like a city or school district, a MUD can levy a property tax and issue bonds. Many MUDs also charge you for water and sewer service through a monthly utility bill, which is separate from the tax.</p>

<h2>Why MUDs are so common in Houston-area subdivisions</h2>
<p>Much of the Houston region's growth has happened in unincorporated parts of Harris, Fort Bend, Montgomery and Waller counties, or in city extraterritorial jurisdictions where the city does not provide utilities. Before a builder can sell homes, someone has to pay for water plants, sewer lines, detention ponds and streets. A MUD lets that cost be financed over decades instead of being added to the price of each lot up front.</p>
<p>The typical path looks like this:</p>
<ol>
<li>A developer petitions to create a district covering the land it plans to build on.</li>
<li>The developer advances money to build the first water, sewer and drainage facilities.</li>
<li>Voters in the district (often very few people at first) authorize bonds.</li>
<li>As homes are built and taxable value grows, the MUD sells bonds and reimburses the developer.</li>
<li>Homeowners repay those bonds through the MUD tax over the following decades.</li>
</ol>
<p>That is why master-planned communities in Katy, Cypress and Fulshear are frequently split into several numbered MUDs, each with its own rate.</p>

<h2>How MUD bonds and tax rates work</h2>
<p>Like other Texas taxing units, a MUD sets its tax rate in dollars per $100 of taxable value. The rate usually has two parts:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Debt service.</strong> The portion that pays principal and interest on the district's bonds. This is usually the larger share in a newer MUD.</li>
<li><strong>Maintenance and operations.</strong> The portion that pays to run the district: plant operations, repairs, contracts and administration.</li>
</ul>
<p>The math is the same as any property tax. A home with a taxable value of $350,000 in a MUD with a $0.80 rate pays $350,000 divided by 100, times $0.80, or $2,800 a year to the MUD. A MUD can also adopt its own homestead exemption, which lowers your taxable value for MUD purposes, so file your <a href="/blog/texas-homestead-exemption/">homestead exemption</a> and check whether your district offers one.</p>
<h3>Why MUD rates often fall over time</h3>
<p>In a brand-new district, a small number of finished homes carries the debt for infrastructure that will eventually serve the whole community. As more homes and businesses are built, the tax base grows and the district can often cover the same debt payment with a lower rate. As older bonds are paid off, the debt portion can fall further.</p>
<p>That trend is common, not guaranteed. A district can issue additional bonds that voters already authorized, refinance debt, or raise its operations rate for repairs. Look at a MUD's history over several years instead of assuming the rate will drop.</p>

<h2>How to find a MUD's tax rate before you buy</h2>
<p>You do not have to guess. Several sources show which MUD a property is in and what it charges:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Your county appraisal district.</strong> Search the address on HCAD, Fort Bend CAD, Montgomery CAD or your county's district. The account page lists every taxing jurisdiction, including the MUD, and its rate.</li>
<li><strong>The county tax office or the MUD's collector.</strong> Some MUDs are billed through the county tax office, while others use a private tax assessor-collector and send a separate bill. The MUD's website usually says which.</li>
<li><strong>The Comptroller's database.</strong> The Texas Comptroller's Special Purpose District Public Information Database lists districts' self-reported tax rates and outstanding bonds. The data is not independently verified, so confirm key numbers with the district.</li>
<li><strong>The seller's notice to purchasers.</strong> This one is required by law, as explained below.</li>
</ul>
<h3>The notice to purchasers (Texas Water Code §49.452)</h3>
<p>Texas Water Code §49.452 requires a seller of property in a MUD and similar districts to give the buyer a written notice before the buyer signs a binding contract. The buyer signs it to acknowledge receipt, and an updated copy is signed at closing and recorded in the county deed records.</p>
<p>The form, set out in §49.4521, must state:</p>
<ul>
<li>The district's current tax rate per $100 of value, or a projected rate if it has not taxed yet.</li>
<li>The total bonds voters have approved, broken out by water, sewer and drainage, roads, and parks.</li>
<li>The total bonds the district has actually issued.</li>
<li>Any standby fee on undeveloped lots.</li>
<li>Whether the district lies in a city's extraterritorial jurisdiction or overlaps a city.</li>
<li>The district's purpose, with a statement that the cost of its facilities is not included in the purchase price.</li>
</ul>
<p>The gap between bonds approved and bonds issued is the number to study. A large unissued authorization means the district can still borrow more, which can keep the rate from falling.</p>
<p>If the seller fails to give the notice before the contract is signed, the buyer may generally terminate the contract. A buyer who receives the notice at or before closing and chooses to close is presumed to waive that right. Talk to a real estate attorney if a notice was missed or wrong.</p>

<h2>Example: comparing total tax rates</h2>
<p>MUD rates only make sense in the context of your total tax rate. The table below uses round, illustrative numbers, not any specific neighborhood's rates, to show how three similar $400,000 homes can compare. Exemptions are ignored to keep the math simple.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Taxing unit</th><th>Home A: inside a city</th><th>Home B: newer MUD</th><th>Home C: mature MUD</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>School district</td><td>$1.00</td><td>$1.00</td><td>$1.00</td></tr>
<tr><td>County and related units</td><td>$0.45</td><td>$0.45</td><td>$0.45</td></tr>
<tr><td>City</td><td>$0.55</td><td>None</td><td>None</td></tr>
<tr><td>MUD</td><td>None</td><td>$0.90</td><td>$0.35</td></tr>
<tr><td>Emergency services district</td><td>None</td><td>$0.10</td><td>$0.10</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Total rate per $100</strong></td><td><strong>$2.00</strong></td><td><strong>$2.45</strong></td><td><strong>$1.90</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Annual tax on $400,000</strong></td><td><strong>$8,000</strong></td><td><strong>$9,800</strong></td><td><strong>$7,600</strong></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>A MUD does not automatically mean higher taxes. A mature district can be cheaper than city taxes, while a new district in an early phase can be noticeably more expensive. Plug real rates from the appraisal district into our <a href="/tools/texas-property-tax-calculator/">Texas property tax calculator</a>, then run the payment through the <a href="/tools/mortgage-calculator/">mortgage calculator</a>, because a higher tax rate can shrink what you qualify for.</p>
[[tool:texas-property-tax-calculator]]

<h2>MUD vs PID vs city</h2>
<p>Buyers in new communities often see a MUD and a PID on the same disclosure package. They are different tools:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>MUD</th><th>PID</th><th>City</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Created by</td><td>Petition, under the Water Code or a special act</td><td>A city or county (Local Government Code Ch. 372 or 382)</td><td>Incorporation by voters</td></tr>
<tr><td>Charge</td><td>Property tax on appraised value</td><td>Special assessment on benefited property</td><td>Property tax, plus sales tax</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pays for</td><td>Water, sewer, drainage; sometimes roads and parks</td><td>Specific authorized improvements</td><td>Police, fire, streets, utilities, parks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Can you pay it off?</td><td>No</td><td>Usually yes, at any time</td><td>No</td></tr>
<tr><td>Buyer notice</td><td>Water Code §49.452</td><td>Property Code §5.014</td><td>None required</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>A PID assessment is set each year in the city's or county's annual service plan update, and unpaid installments can lead to penalties, interest and a lien. Ask for the remaining PID balance before you write an offer.</p>
<p>Annexation is the other piece. When a city annexes all of a MUD's territory, the city generally takes over the district's assets and debts and the MUD is dissolved, so the MUD tax disappears and a city tax replaces it. Since 2019, Texas law has required consent for annexations across all cities, so annexing an entire suburban MUD is less common than it once was.</p>

<h2>Pros and cons of buying in a MUD</h2>
<h3>Pros</h3>
<ul>
<li>Newer infrastructure, often with detention and drainage designed to current standards.</li>
<li>Amenities such as trails, parks and lakes that a MUD or PID may help fund.</li>
<li>No city tax line in unincorporated areas, which can offset part of the MUD tax.</li>
<li>Local control: residents elect the board and can attend its public meetings.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Cons</h3>
<ul>
<li>Higher total tax rates in young districts, which raise your monthly escrow payment.</li>
<li>Uncertainty if a district has large unissued bond authority.</li>
<li>Small boards that draw little public attention, so it pays to read meeting minutes and audits.</li>
<li>Services like police may come from the county or a contract rather than a city department.</li>
</ul>
<p>Tax treatment and contract remedies depend on your situation, so confirm details with a CPA or real estate attorney before you rely on them.</p>
